WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.360
Hold on, before we begin this video, there’s&nbsp;
something really important we need you to do. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.000
remember this phrase, while you’re watching it’s&nbsp;
critical that you don’t let these four words slip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.040
out of your head, even for a split second. You can&nbsp;
even pause the video and repeat the sentence to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.000
yourself a few times, just to get it to fully&nbsp;
sink in. Remember, please: Belief is the key.&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.960
We have all wondered at one point or another,&nbsp;
“what happens after?” After our lives draw to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.120
their inevitable close, and we take our&nbsp;
last breath in the land of the living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.240
what happens to us? It’s a question that has&nbsp;
baffled scientists and philosophers all over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.640
world for almost as long as human civilization&nbsp;
has existed: What happens after we die? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.680
the answer to that particular question normally&nbsp;
varies depending on who you choose to ask.&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.800
There are, as you probably already know, numerous&nbsp;
beliefs held by all religions and cultures around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.840
the globe, that there exists an afterlife.&nbsp;
While the description of the afterlife is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.040
dependent on the religious or cultural group it&nbsp;
is linked to, the concept usually revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.600
the soul leaving the body after death, and living&nbsp;
forever in eternal paradise. That is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.560
unless you’ve committed sin during your lifetime,&nbsp;
which normally results in being sent down to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.720
eternity in punishment instead of paradise.
But sadly, there’s no way to prove which, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.440
any of the various afterlives, actually exist. By&nbsp;
their very nature, these afterlives exist separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.200
from the real, physical world that we live in&nbsp;
while we are still alive. Stuck here on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.960
we can only observe what we see happening after&nbsp;
death, and what we assume it might be like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.800
After all, it’s not like a dead person&nbsp;
can tell us what being dead is like…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.000
well, under usual circumstances anyway.
With no proof of the afterlife’s existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.040
we do unfortunately get left with the bleak&nbsp;
and existentially horrifying assumption that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.200
when we die, there is nothing waiting for us on&nbsp;
the other side, no light at the end of the tunnel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.680
no pearly gates, just… nothing, forever. Of&nbsp;
course, being unconscious for eternity isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.080
quite as bad as the other possibility.
What could that other possibility be?&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.360
Well, according to the SCP Foundation’s entry&nbsp;
for SCP-2718, you might not want to know. Anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.120
attempting to access the data file on SCP-2718&nbsp;
will immediately notice a few details that set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.280
it apart from others contained in the Foundation’s&nbsp;
archive. Firstly, an ever-changing SCP number - It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.800
perpetually swaps numbers with other files to keep&nbsp;
itself hidden. Next, a unique object class that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.080
only found in this entry. Instead of the Keter&nbsp;
or Euclid or Safe class that you might expect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.960
you’ll read ‘dammerung’, a German word&nbsp;
that loosely translates to either dusk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.640
dawn, twilight, or nightfall.
You might also spot the anomaly’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.120
special containment procedures, mentioning&nbsp;
that this page of the SCP database has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.760
specially constructed in such a way that&nbsp;
makes it as difficult as possible to access.&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.240
The entry itself even begs the person&nbsp;
reading it not to go any further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.840
not to look at the description of SCP-2718.&nbsp;
Now, we all know that the Foundation goes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.600
great lengths to keep some things a secret,&nbsp;
but why all the added measures for SCP-2718?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.400
It might well be that the information contained&nbsp;
behind all this extra security could change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.080
lives of anyone that reads it. Or, to put it&nbsp;
more accurately, it could change the afterlives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.800
of whoever learns what SCP-2718 really is.
This anomaly isn’t a nightmarish creature or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.920
an object with a strange function, it’s not&nbsp;
an entity from outside our universe or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:43.280
a person with an unusual origin. SCP-2718 is a&nbsp;
cognitohazard, a piece of information that poses a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:48.880
direct danger to anyone that is able to understand&nbsp;
it. That’s why the Foundation has so many pleas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.320
to not read any further, they’re not doing that&nbsp;
to protect the organization and their secrets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.000
but to try and protect the reader themselves.
“All personnel, regardless of clearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.160
are forbidden to expose themselves to&nbsp;
the Description of this article under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.720
any circumstances… Do not discuss the existence&nbsp;
of this article with any person. No disciplinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.880
action will be necessary, provided you close&nbsp;
this article now, and clear your browser cache.”&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:23.200
Sounds serious, right? So serious, in fact, that&nbsp;
anyone trying to learn about SCP-2718 is actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.640
discouraged to do so, even members of Foundation&nbsp;
personnel are offered exemption from punishment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.160
if they just do what they’re told, and turn away.
But you still want to know, don’t you?&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.640
Alright, don’t say we didn’t try our best&nbsp;
to warn you. You’ve ignored all the signs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:45.040
the repeated warnings to not look any further into&nbsp;
SCP-2718, to not look at its description or pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:51.680
that information on to anyone else. No, you just&nbsp;
had to keep digging deeper and deeper, didn’t you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.560
Searching for the answers you’re going to wish&nbsp;
you had never gone looking for in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.320
Push far enough through the security measures the&nbsp;
Foundation have placed around the SCP-2718 file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:08.000
and you’ll find a transcribed audio recording&nbsp;
from none other than a member of the O5 Council.&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.640
The councilwoman Miriam Prayther, O5-7, speaks&nbsp;
to a subordinate who begins the recording. As it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.360
starts, she talks about how the various members of&nbsp;
the O5 Council have used various anomalous means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:25.200
and sometimes other SCPs, to artificially extend&nbsp;
their lifespans. The council is functionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.720
immortal. Boring, right? Anyone who knows enough&nbsp;
about the SCP Foundation already knows that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.480
functional immortality is what has kept the O5s in&nbsp;
power for so many decades. But what you might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:42.960
know, is there was one council member who didn’t.
His name was Roger Sheldon. Despite being offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.160
each of the nineteen known methods of extending&nbsp;
life that the other overseers had gladly used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.440
Sheldon refused each and every one. Instead,&nbsp;
he would often go on vacations away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.800
Foundation, without announcing his intention to&nbsp;
do so. Both doing this and refusing to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.440
immortal in some way are actions that are now&nbsp;
forbidden for any member of the O5 Council,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.680
presumably because of Roger.
On one of his impromptu trips away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.560
Roger Sheldon died from a stroke. Given that he&nbsp;
didn’t let anyone know where he was planning on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.080
going, he was sadly left to rot after his death.&nbsp;
However, Roger possessed an important anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.600
artifact in his possession at all times, meaning&nbsp;
that the SCP Foundation was keen to recover his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.040
body and by extension, this object. He&nbsp;
also had knowledge of a unique keyword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.400
a phrase referred to as a shibboleth, which the&nbsp;
other overseers needed to extract from Sheldon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:40.080
But how do you get information from a dead man?&nbsp;
Well, you bring him back to life, of course.&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.720
And we’re not talking about the normal kind of&nbsp;
“resurrection” the Foundation often indulges in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.880
which is really just glorified cloning. This is&nbsp;
the ripping of an actual soul back from the void,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.240
and placing it inside its body once more&nbsp;
so it can live again as it once did.&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.200
Eventually, after fourteen years of searching,&nbsp;
Roger Sheldon’s remains were exhumed and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.320
returned to the Foundation. There, his personal&nbsp;
belongings were recovered, including the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:12.240
mysterious artifact. Then, just as planned, they&nbsp;
revived Roger in order to recover the shibboleth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.520
from his memory. The O5 Council had commissioned&nbsp;
the creation of a brand new body for Roger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:23.200
one that perfectly matched his old one in every&nbsp;
conceivable detail. And it worked. What was odd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.160
was that the formerly deceased overseer, who had&nbsp;
refused to extend his lifespan and live forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.960
seemed overjoyed to be brought back from the dead.
The resurrection seemed to work exactly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.400
intended, even better in fact, with Roger now&nbsp;
firmly back among the living. He appeared to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.960
a newfound interest in preserving human life,&nbsp;
and empathy for others that he’d never shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.080
before. Sheldon was concerned about personnel&nbsp;
safety and containment protocols in a way he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.840
hadn’t been before his stroke. But this apparent&nbsp;
compassion was underscored by something else:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:58.640
caution. Roger never went anywhere without&nbsp;
an entourage of medical staff and bodyguards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.360
almost frightened of how fragile life was.
You might think there’s nothing too alarming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.640
about that behavior, that maybe death had given&nbsp;
Sheldon a new perspective, an appreciation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:15.520
the delicate nature of human life. But it was&nbsp;
more than that, it was an unprecedented fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:21.760
a phobia of allowing people, anyone, to die.
Then came Roger Sheldon’s most unpredictable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:27.440
course of action. Acting against all regulations&nbsp;
and Foundation protocols, he secretly rushed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:33.520
the containment chamber of SCP-106. This anomaly&nbsp;
is a humanoid that secretes a black goop from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:39.120
body, capable of dragging human beings into a&nbsp;
pocket dimension that it controls and uses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.880
torture its victims. Sheldon was trying to make&nbsp;
some kind of bargain with SCP-106 when he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.080
detained by Miriam Prayther. When told to explain&nbsp;
his actions, Roger Sheldon eventually gave them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:56.000
his horrifying testimony, the reason he’d been&nbsp;
so afraid and so cautious after his resurrection:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.640
he knew what happened after death.
Cast your mind back to the start of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.080
this video, all that talk about afterlives and&nbsp;
what really happens to us after we die. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:11.200
there is one answer to that question that&nbsp;
doesn’t often cross people’s minds. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.240
everyone who ponders the question ‘what happens&nbsp;
after’ subconsciously tries to overlook it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.280
because to even think it’s possible&nbsp;
presents a whole horde of horrors in itself.&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:27.520
What could be such a terrifying thought, that we&nbsp;
all hope it simply isn’t true? It’s the simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.840
horrible possibility that has hardly&nbsp;
occurred to anyone throughout human history:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:38.160
that the dead remain conscious after their&nbsp;
bodies have shut down. They remain aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.560
everything that happens to them, they are&nbsp;
able to feel themselves slowly decaying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:51.760
bones and skin withering away, buried under the&nbsp;
cold, wasting away into nothingness day after day.&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:58.320
That is what SCP-2718 is.
After suffering his stroke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.000
Roger Sheldon explained how he could&nbsp;
still feel everything happening to him.&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:08.080
“My skin blistered and split in the sunlight;&nbsp;
biting insects descended rapidly. I felt eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.760
hatch, larvae crawl, gases build and burst&nbsp;
within me, individual cells rupturing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.280
interstitial fluids souring and blackening.&nbsp;
Somehow my capacity to experience and store these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:24.880
sensations grew… I was aware of every fingernail&nbsp;
and strand of hair that pulled away in the wind…”&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.520
He went on, describing every horror he’d&nbsp;
experienced in lurid detail as his body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:35.520
broke down in real-time, knowing that those same&nbsp;
images and thoughts would never leave his head.&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.760
Shocked by the revelation, Miriam and the&nbsp;
other Overseer rushed to hold a meeting of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:45.040
the rest of the O5 Council, wherein Roger once&nbsp;
again described his posthumous experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:50.320
Hearing what happens after death drove every&nbsp;
council member mad, each reacting with shock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.360
stress, and anger. One of them tried to calm the&nbsp;
rest down, while another motioned to treat death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:01.520
itself as a Keter class SCP and neutralize it. The&nbsp;
chaos was eventually quelled when O5-1 silenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:07.440
the other O5s, activating Emergency Protocol 17&nbsp;
to gas the overseers with amnestics to wipe their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.640
memories of what they had learned. Only Roger&nbsp;
and -7 were able to escape the room before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:18.000
bulkhead sealed and the gas was deployed.
Roger believed that people had a right to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:24.400
know about SCP-2718. Agreeing with him, Miriam&nbsp;
created a log of all she knew about what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:30.880
after death, then permanently locked it within the&nbsp;
SCP database, protected from ever being deleted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:36.480
or having its description altered. However, on&nbsp;
the orders of O5-1, Miriam was gunned down by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:43.120
Foundation personnel, leaving Roger to escape.&nbsp;
Presumably, he returned to SCP-106, agreeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:49.280
to be tortured forever in the creature’s pocket&nbsp;
dimension. To him, that was preferable to dying.&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:56.160
So, if you now know what SCP-2718 is, then why&nbsp;
did we start this video with the phrase ‘Belief is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:02.400
the key’? Well, as we said earlier, this anomaly&nbsp;
is a cognitohazard, it’s a phenomenon that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:08.000
occurs after you are exposed to a certain piece&nbsp;
of information. That knowledge is what is being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:14.000
contained here, within the entry left by O5-7.&nbsp;
You see, it’s actually knowing what happens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:20.880
you after death that makes SCP-2718 happen.
If you spend your life believing in a version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:26.480
of the afterlife, only to then learn&nbsp;
about SCP-2718, then that information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.840
will override whatever a religion, culture,&nbsp;
or faith led you to believe about death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:40.000
Knowing about SCP-2718 overwrites your afterlife.&nbsp;
It creates a new, endless and meaningless pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:47.920
as you experience every sensation beyond your&nbsp;
death. Decay, decomposition, cremation. That’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:55.360
reason that belief is the key, because once you&nbsp;
are exposed to it, SCP-2718 becomes what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:03.120
after you die. In our defense: We warned you...
Now go check out “SCP–2935 – O, Death” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:09.520
“SCP-657 - Death-predicting Man” for more&nbsp;
anomalies about Death: The true final frontier!

